1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,900
What numbers let a program describe a
targeting line or an object size?

2
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,860
We will find origins and directions in
grappling footage,

3
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,420
then distinguish lines, segments and rays.

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,740
Next we will represent round objects with
spheres

5
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:18,960
and calculate a box enclosing a set of points.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:22,520
Finally we will rotate a box, expose a common
error,

7
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,100
and derive a safe transformation formula.

8
00:00:27,516 --> 00:00:29,150
We travel among rocks in A Story About My
Uncle.

9
00:00:29,370 --> 00:00:31,590
Watch the bright connection extending from the
hand.

10
00:00:32,966 --> 00:00:38,206
When it appears, distinguish its origin near
the hand from its attachment on a rock.

11
00:00:39,562 --> 00:00:42,862
Aiming at another rock changes the connection
direction.

12
00:00:43,182 --> 00:00:46,362
One starting point alone does not determine a
line.

13
00:00:47,638 --> 00:00:51,078
Its screen length depends on the camera and
perspective;

14
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:54,058
pixels are not automatically world distance.

15
00:00:55,354 --> 00:00:58,674
Remember how the endpoints relate as we move.

16
00:00:58,674 --> 00:01:02,094
Our diagram will calculate that relation in
explicitly chosen coordinates.

17
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:05,590
The visible grapple motivates the mathematics;

18
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:10,050
it does not reveal this game ray tests or
physics implementation.

19
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:14,210
We can describe the same circle differently
depending on the calculation.

20
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:18,310
Storing its center and radius gives direct
shape data.

21
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:22,390
A parametric representation constructs a point
by adding

22
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:26,230
radius times cosine and sine of an angle to
the center.

23
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:29,610
An implicit representation compares squared
distance

24
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:32,950
from a candidate point to the center with
squared radius.

25
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:36,690
Equality means the boundary; a smaller squared
distance means the interior.

26
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:39,690
Watch the classification change as the point
moves.

27
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:44,190
Parametric equations conveniently construct
points;

28
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:46,590
implicit equations conveniently test them.
Neither is always better.

29
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:49,730
An angle parameter does not automatically
represent time.

30
00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:53,610
Define whether a parameter means angle, time
or distance.

31
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:59,030
For a circle at the origin, x squared plus y
squared equals radius squared.

32
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,570
Translating its center changes the equation.

33
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:07,870
Take center two, three and radius one.

34
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:11,730
Subtract two from x and three from y before
squaring.

35
00:02:11,930 --> 00:02:18,030
Expansion gives x squared plus y squared minus
four x minus six y plus twelve equal to zero.

36
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:20,750
A general conic therefore includes linear and

37
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:23,690
constant terms as well as squared and cross
terms.

38
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,890
A circle needs two center components and one
radius.

39
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:29,790
A sphere needs three center components and one
radius.

40
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,510
Different radii along the axes describe an
ellipse or ellipsoid.

41
00:02:33,790 --> 00:02:37,190
Nonuniformly stretching a sphere generally
changes it into an ellipsoid.

42
00:02:37,817 --> 00:02:40,977
We connect to another rock and travel through
the air.

43
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:46,279
Find both endpoints whenever the beam appears.

44
00:02:46,619 --> 00:02:48,487
Subtracting origin from attachment gives a

45
00:02:48,487 --> 00:02:51,159
displacement vector containing direction and
length.

46
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:58,301
Even when two rocks lie in the same direction,
their displacement lengths differ.

47
00:03:00,683 --> 00:03:03,183
For direction alone, divide displacement by
its length.

48
00:03:03,343 --> 00:03:06,843
Store a unit direction and distance
separately.

49
00:03:09,105 --> 00:03:14,545
The hand-to-rock connection and the character
actual path are different questions.

50
00:03:14,745 --> 00:03:18,385
Forces and trajectories belong to later
dynamics lessons.

51
00:03:20,847 --> 00:03:23,567
Focus here on geometric descriptions between
positions.

52
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:29,047
A fractional parameter and a distance
parameter have different meanings.

53
00:03:29,667 --> 00:03:33,907
Call the origin the letter o and displacement
delta.

54
00:03:34,227 --> 00:03:37,407
Position is the origin plus t times delta.

55
00:03:37,747 --> 00:03:40,847
Zero gives the origin and one gives the
endpoint.

56
00:03:41,107 --> 00:03:44,187
Restricting t to zero through one defines the
segment.

57
00:03:44,487 --> 00:03:48,367
Allowing every real t produces an infinite
line.

58
00:03:48,687 --> 00:03:50,687
Negative t extends in the opposite direction.

59
00:03:50,867 --> 00:03:53,807
A mathematical ray usually restricts t to

60
00:03:53,887 --> 00:03:55,747
nonnegative values and extends infinitely
forward.

61
00:03:56,087 --> 00:03:59,327
The book also calls a finite directed segment
a ray. Engine ray queries

62
00:03:59,327 --> 00:04:04,247
may have a maximum distance, so inspect the
actual allowed domain.

63
00:04:04,387 --> 00:04:08,847
Record the origin, direction and parameter
range together,

64
00:04:09,027 --> 00:04:11,667
rather than relying on a name.

65
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:17,590
Let the origin be two, one and displacement
six, three. Where is the midpoint?

66
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:23,970
At t equal to one half, add three, one point
five. The point is five, two point five.

67
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:30,650
This one half is a fraction of the segment,
not half a second or half a meter.

68
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:33,210
The displacement length is square root of
forty-five.

69
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:36,950
Divide displacement by that length to obtain a
unit direction.

70
00:04:37,570 --> 00:04:42,210
Multiplying that unit direction by s makes s a
distance.

71
00:04:42,350 --> 00:04:45,850
The same midpoint uses s equal to half of
square root forty-five.

72
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:50,450
Identical endpoints give zero displacement.
Check before normalization

73
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:53,290
and define whether to treat the degenerate
input as a point or reject it.

74
00:04:53,917 --> 00:04:56,737
We aim at rocks above and below us.

75
00:04:57,097 --> 00:05:00,317
The beam looks vertical in some views and
diagonal in others.

76
00:05:02,897 --> 00:05:04,877
Both are ordinary lines, yet a slope-only

77
00:05:05,337 --> 00:05:08,857
representation has a problem at vertical
lines.

78
00:05:09,177 --> 00:05:16,497
When the x change is zero, we cannot divide
the y change by it.

79
00:05:19,157 --> 00:05:23,517
Our diagram will use a normal and offset
instead.

80
00:05:23,857 --> 00:05:26,457
A normal is perpendicular to the line
direction.

81
00:05:29,317 --> 00:05:31,977
Screen vertical comes from the camera. For
calculation,

82
00:05:32,037 --> 00:05:37,117
first choose the coordinate frame defining x
and y.

83
00:05:39,557 --> 00:05:42,997
We will also find points equidistant from two
sites.

84
00:05:43,457 --> 00:05:47,297
Their connecting line and perpendicular
bisector are different lines.

85
00:05:47,917 --> 00:05:52,077
The familiar slope-intercept equation needs a
different form,

86
00:05:52,357 --> 00:05:56,037
x equal to a constant, for a vertical line.

87
00:05:56,237 --> 00:05:59,117
Using normal n dotted with a point equal to
offset d

88
00:05:59,117 --> 00:06:02,677
represents vertical and horizontal lines in
one form.

89
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:07,857
The normal is perpendicular to the line. A
zero normal does not define a valid line.

90
00:06:08,177 --> 00:06:11,857
For points equidistant from zero, zero and
two, zero,

91
00:06:12,117 --> 00:06:17,397
use displacement two, zero as normal and
midpoint one, zero on the bisector.

92
00:06:17,397 --> 00:06:23,337
The equation is two x equals two, or x equals
one. This vertical line bisects the two sites.

93
00:06:23,517 --> 00:06:29,377
When normalizing n, divide d by the same
length. Scaling only n moves the line;

94
00:06:29,657 --> 00:06:33,817
the residual is directly a signed distance
only for a unit normal.

95
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:36,890
In Serious Sam2, round objects approach and
bright explosions appear.

96
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:41,530
First find the rounded outlines.

97
00:06:42,038 --> 00:06:45,898
A center and radius offer a simple calculation

98
00:06:46,138 --> 00:06:49,566
model: the same distance in every direction.

99
00:06:49,886 --> 00:06:55,066
A rounded visual effect is not proof of a
spherical surface or damage region.

100
00:06:55,386 --> 00:06:58,186
Radius belongs to explicitly defined
calculation data.

101
00:06:58,734 --> 00:07:01,954
A target closer than the radius is inside;
equal distance is on the boundary.

102
00:07:01,954 --> 00:07:05,874
Our diagram will distinguish three cases.

103
00:07:06,782 --> 00:07:11,382
Growing screen size as an object approaches is
different from changing its actual radius.

104
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:14,522
Perspective pixels do not establish world
size.

105
00:07:15,070 --> 00:07:18,010
A sphere is an initial approximation for a
complex rounded object.

106
00:07:18,370 --> 00:07:21,590
Specify what must be enclosed and the
acceptable approximation error.

107
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:25,630
A sphere has three center components and a
radius.

108
00:07:25,890 --> 00:07:28,990
Compute squared length of point minus center.

109
00:07:29,350 --> 00:07:35,530
Take center one, two, three and radius two.
Squared radius is four.

110
00:07:35,910 --> 00:07:41,290
At the center, squared distance is zero:
inside because it is smaller than four.

111
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:46,590
At three, two, three, the x displacement is
two.

112
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:48,730
Squared distance four puts the point on the
surface.

113
00:07:48,730 --> 00:07:52,610
At four, two, three, squared distance is nine:
outside.

114
00:07:53,050 --> 00:07:57,430
No square root is needed for this comparison.

115
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:03,490
Equality defines the surface; less than or
equal defines the solid.

116
00:08:03,870 --> 00:08:08,810
Validate finite coordinates, a nonnegative
radius and a tolerance with defined units.

117
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:15,380
Doubling radius doubles diameter, but area and
volume grow differently.

118
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:21,020
Diameter is two r and circumference is two pi
r. Both have length units.

119
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:22,960
Circle area is pi r squared; sphere surface
area is four pi r squared.

120
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:27,620
Both use squared length units.

121
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:30,440
Sphere volume is four thirds pi r cubed.

122
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:34,240
Cubic units make volume grow eightfold when
radius doubles.

123
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:37,060
Surface area grows fourfold. Calling length,

124
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:41,200
area and volume simply size can cause
incorrect comparisons.

125
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:46,220
When changing a game radius, distinguish what
remains constant. Effects, tests and particle

126
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:52,160
counts are design choices; these formulas
alone do not determine game performance.

127
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:56,633
Small and large enemies move through the
village.

128
00:08:56,633 --> 00:09:00,453
Imagine checking every head, arm and torso
detail.

129
00:09:00,753 --> 00:09:05,089
Detailed comparisons across poses involve many
data points.

130
00:09:05,289 --> 00:09:08,805
Simple bounding shapes can first filter
candidates.

131
00:09:09,105 --> 00:09:12,885
Think of a sphere around a round head or a box
around a tall body.

132
00:09:13,245 --> 00:09:17,565
These are our illustrative proxies, not
verified game colliders.

133
00:09:17,605 --> 00:09:22,081
Distinguish a box following object orientation
from a box parallel to world axes.

134
00:09:22,081 --> 00:09:25,181
They can enclose different amounts of empty
space.

135
00:09:25,897 --> 00:09:28,937
Turning or extending arms changes what must be
enclosed.

136
00:09:29,417 --> 00:09:33,497
A bound built for one static pose may not
contain every animation pose.

137
00:09:34,253 --> 00:09:36,973
Our diagram will simplify the shape to a point
set and

138
00:09:37,393 --> 00:09:39,993
independently find minimum and maximum along
three axes.

139
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:45,253
An axis-aligned bounding box, or AABB,

140
00:09:45,253 --> 00:09:48,333
encloses points with six faces parallel to x,
y and z.

141
00:09:48,613 --> 00:09:52,533
Store each axis minimum and maximum.

142
00:09:53,013 --> 00:09:56,533
The three minima need not belong to one actual
input point.

143
00:09:56,853 --> 00:09:58,853
Center is the average of minimum and maximum.

144
00:09:58,907 --> 00:10:03,833
Half-extents are half their difference;
distinguish them from full size.

145
00:10:04,013 --> 00:10:06,993
For membership, compare x with its x interval,

146
00:10:06,993 --> 00:10:10,773
y with its y interval and z with its z
interval.

147
00:10:10,833 --> 00:10:13,453
All three tests must pass. A copy error
comparing y against

148
00:10:14,213 --> 00:10:19,353
x bounds can compile while giving incorrect
results.

149
00:10:19,453 --> 00:10:22,413
An oriented bounding box, or OBB, follows
object orientation.

150
00:10:22,793 --> 00:10:27,613
Extra orientation data can reduce empty space
around a slender shape.

151
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:32,690
Use the book five points and compare one
coordinate axis at a time.

152
00:10:33,010 --> 00:10:35,490
Initialize both extrema from the first point.

153
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:40,650
For each next point, update each component
minimum or maximum.

154
00:10:40,650 --> 00:10:44,710
Follow the highlighted code and matching table
colors.

155
00:10:44,830 --> 00:10:51,090
The final minimum is minus five, minus seven,
minus five; maximum is seven, eleven, eight.

156
00:10:51,410 --> 00:10:56,070
Center is one, two, one point five. Full size
is twelve,

157
00:10:56,070 --> 00:11:00,750
eighteen, thirteen; half-extents are six,
nine, six point five.

158
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:06,470
Initializing at zero can wrongly include the
origin when every point lies on one side.

159
00:11:06,810 --> 00:11:09,950
Use a first valid point or an explicit
empty-box state.

160
00:11:10,110 --> 00:11:13,670
Handle empty input, infinity and NaN first.

161
00:11:13,970 --> 00:11:17,790
Match the iteration bounds to the actual array
length.

162
00:11:18,466 --> 00:11:22,726
Large and small enemies with extended arms
move across the scene.

163
00:11:22,946 --> 00:11:26,126
Imagine enclosing each body with a simple box.

164
00:11:27,422 --> 00:11:31,382
Box corners contain space outside the body.

165
00:11:31,622 --> 00:11:34,622
The boxes can overlap while the actual bodies
remain apart.

166
00:11:35,958 --> 00:11:40,818
World-axis boxes around slender or tilted
shapes can contain

167
00:11:41,098 --> 00:11:43,858
substantial empty space. Focus on outline
versus enclosure.

168
00:11:45,354 --> 00:11:50,174
The earlier2D box-overlap lecture supplies a
fast candidate test.

169
00:11:50,174 --> 00:11:52,554
Remember the limit of its result.

170
00:11:53,990 --> 00:11:56,710
After filtering, use a test accurate enough
for the game rule and cost.

171
00:11:57,050 --> 00:12:01,950
Choose proxies deliberately.

172
00:12:03,566 --> 00:12:06,366
Our next diagram gives two separated slender
rectangles whose bounding boxes overlap,

173
00:12:06,746 --> 00:12:11,386
distinguishing candidates from actual shape
contact.

174
00:12:12,466 --> 00:12:15,726
Tilt two slender rectangles in the same
direction.

175
00:12:15,966 --> 00:12:18,566
A visible gap separates their actual shapes.

176
00:12:18,886 --> 00:12:21,746
Their axis-aligned boxes combine independent x
and y ranges,

177
00:12:21,746 --> 00:12:24,966
producing overlapping enclosures.

178
00:12:25,126 --> 00:12:28,826
Box overlap means a candidate for further
testing,

179
00:12:29,106 --> 00:12:31,466
not confirmed contact of detailed shapes.

180
00:12:31,706 --> 00:12:36,246
Nonoverlapping valid bounds rule out overlap
of contained shapes.

181
00:12:36,246 --> 00:12:40,506
This requires each bound to contain the entire
current shape.

182
00:12:40,506 --> 00:12:45,506
Spheres retain their shape under rotation;
oriented boxes may fit slender shapes better.

183
00:12:45,926 --> 00:12:49,886
A tighter bound is not automatically cheaper
overall.

184
00:12:50,366 --> 00:12:54,446
Compare update cost, candidate count and
subsequent test cost.

185
00:12:54,766 --> 00:12:59,126
This counterexample explains the role of
bounds rather than an entire collision engine.

186
00:12:59,883 --> 00:13:03,683
How do we rebuild bounds after rotating a box?

187
00:13:03,903 --> 00:13:07,843
First try transforming only its minimum and
maximum.

188
00:13:08,323 --> 00:13:12,683
Rotate a square with x and y from minus one to
one by forty-five degrees.

189
00:13:12,983 --> 00:13:16,443
The two diagonal endpoints move to a vertical
line at x zero.

190
00:13:16,583 --> 00:13:20,083
Using only those points falsely gives zero
width.

191
00:13:20,243 --> 00:13:23,743
The other corners extend to plus and minus
square root two.

192
00:13:24,063 --> 00:13:27,523
Rotation changes which corner supplies each
extremum.

193
00:13:27,523 --> 00:13:30,743
An old minimum does not necessarily remain a
minimum.

194
00:13:30,827 --> 00:13:37,243
For a3D box, transform all eight corners and
recompute each coordinate minimum and maximum.

195
00:13:37,583 --> 00:13:40,083
This encloses the transformed input box.

196
00:13:40,403 --> 00:13:45,863
It can differ from bounds constructed by
transforming only the original object points.

197
00:13:46,483 --> 00:13:49,023
We walk through wooden posts and rails,

198
00:13:49,263 --> 00:13:53,363
comparing screen extents of the same
structures from different sides.

199
00:13:53,643 --> 00:13:59,758
An oblique view shortens a long edge and
reveals another face.

200
00:14:00,198 --> 00:14:06,274
These screen changes come from camera
projection.

201
00:14:06,454 --> 00:14:11,474
World bounds use fixed coordinate axes rather
than screen pixels. Changing

202
00:14:11,694 --> 00:14:16,354
only the camera does not automatically change
a stationary object world bounds.

203
00:14:19,409 --> 00:14:24,489
Rotating the object itself in the world can
change which

204
00:14:24,509 --> 00:14:27,729
corner supplies minimum x, as in our square
experiment.

205
00:14:30,704 --> 00:14:34,604
Separate an object-attached box from
world-axis bounds.

206
00:14:35,124 --> 00:14:39,364
First identify whether the camera or the
object is moving.

207
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:46,360
Our next formula handles affine rotation,
scale and translation,

208
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:48,800
without combining them with perspective
projection.

209
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:52,820
With column vectors, an affine transformation
is A times a point plus translation.

210
00:14:52,820 --> 00:14:57,000
Transform the center with that expression.

211
00:14:57,200 --> 00:15:02,160
Half-extents require a different rule. Signed
offsets around the center can combine,

212
00:15:02,353 --> 00:15:06,080
so maximum spread sums absolute contributions.

213
00:15:06,159 --> 00:15:11,200
New half-extents are componentwise absolute A
times original half-extents.

214
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:14,380
This absolute value is not a determinant or
vector norm.

215
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:19,420
Rotating half-extents two, one around z by
forty-five degrees

216
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:23,240
gives both new x and y half-extents three over
square root two.

217
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:27,760
New minimum is transformed center minus new
half-extents,

218
00:15:27,817 --> 00:15:31,480
and maximum is their sum. This matches
transforming all eight box corners.

219
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:36,680
The formula applies to an affine image of the
input box. Do not apply it unchanged

220
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:40,360
to perspective division or claim the tightest
bounds of every contained object.

221
00:15:40,983 --> 00:15:45,003
A long bridge and large enemies share the
scene.

222
00:15:45,003 --> 00:15:48,923
Imagine separate bounds following the bridge
and enclosing moving bodies.

223
00:15:50,179 --> 00:15:55,679
Building a box first and then transforming it
also transforms the box empty space.

224
00:15:56,995 --> 00:16:00,575
Transforming actual object points first and
rebuilding bounds can

225
00:16:00,575 --> 00:16:07,051
exclude different empty regions. The methods
have different inputs.

226
00:16:07,051 --> 00:16:11,291
Define whether you enclose a stationary
bridge,

227
00:16:11,291 --> 00:16:15,831
one body pose or all poses before choosing a
method.

228
00:16:17,087 --> 00:16:19,507
An undersized bound can miss real geometry; an
oversized bound increases

229
00:16:19,507 --> 00:16:25,507
subsequent candidates. Compare accuracy and
calculation cost.

230
00:16:25,507 --> 00:16:31,823
We will finish by comparing both constructions
using the same coordinates,

231
00:16:31,823 --> 00:16:36,363
connecting endpoints, point bounds and
transformed-box bounds.

232
00:16:36,983 --> 00:16:41,563
First problem: origin five, three and
displacement minus seven,

233
00:16:41,563 --> 00:16:43,783
five. What is the endpoint at t one?

234
00:16:44,003 --> 00:16:47,483
Adding gives minus two, eight.

235
00:16:47,483 --> 00:16:52,263
Slope is minus five over seven and intercept
is forty-six over seven.

236
00:16:52,563 --> 00:16:58,283
For the second problem, rotate the five points
around z by forty-five degrees and build

237
00:16:58,283 --> 00:17:02,363
their bounds directly. The x range is minus
six to three, each divided by square root two.

238
00:17:02,363 --> 00:17:06,523
The y range is minus twelve to eighteen,
divided by square root two;

239
00:17:06,523 --> 00:17:11,943
z remains minus five to eight. Transforming
the original box produces a wider enclosure.

240
00:17:12,163 --> 00:17:16,063
Empty space explains why point-set bounds can
differ from transformed-box bounds.

241
00:17:16,483 --> 00:17:20,323
Check the input before interpreting the
result.

242
00:17:20,623 --> 00:17:23,543
We connected shape representations, parameter
domains and units,

243
00:17:23,543 --> 00:17:26,663
sphere tests, and box construction and
transformation.

244
00:17:27,203 --> 00:17:30,923
Next we will calculate plane distances and
positions within triangles.
